(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test06_1A"] = [{"id":"9671d61d","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is from Claude McKay’s 1922 poem “Morning Joy.” The speaker is looking out a window and observing a wold, or large area of land. At night the wide and level stretch of wold, Which at high noon had basked in quiet gold, Far as the eye could see was ghostly white; Dark was the night save for the snow’s weird light. I drew the shades far down, crept into bed; Hearing the cold wind moaning overhead Through the sad pines, my soul, catching its pain, Went sorrowing with it across the plain. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Claude McKay’s 1922 poem “Morning Joy.” The speaker is looking out a window and observing a wold, or large area of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"land.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"At night the wide and level stretch of wold,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Which at high noon had basked in quiet gold,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Far as the eye could see was ghostly white;","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Dark was the night save for the snow’s weird light.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I drew the shades far down, crept into bed;","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Hearing the cold wind moaning overhead","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Through the sad pines, my soul, catching its pain,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Went sorrowing with it across the plain.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “drew” most nearly mean?","choices":["Pulled","Drained","Inspired","Sketched"],"underline":"drew","underline_segments":["drew"]},{"id":"7afb470b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Diadromous fish migrate between freshwater and marine biomes during their life cycle. The migration’s obligate nature is why diadromous fish can be ______ those that are merely euryhaline (able to tolerate high salinity): the euryhaline blackchin tilapia can survive high salinity, but its life cycle does not involve relocation to a different biome, as does that of the diadromous wild salmon.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Diadromous fish migrate between freshwater and marine biomes during their life cycle. The migration’s obligate nature is why diadromous fish","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"can be ______ those that are merely euryhaline (able to tolerate high salinity): the euryhaline blackchin tilapia can survive high salinity, but its life","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cycle does not involve relocation to a different biome, as does that of the diadromous wild salmon.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["demarcated from","reconstituted as","conflated with","derived from"]},{"id":"ae2b3112","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"By combining Indigenous and classical music, Cree composer and cellist Cris Derksen creates works that reflect the diverse cultural landscape of Canada. For her album Orchestral Powwow, Derksen composed new songs in the style of traditional powwow music that were accompanied by classical arrangements played by an orchestra. But where an orchestra would normally follow the directions of a conductor, the musicians on Orchestral Powwow are led by the beat of a powwow drum.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"By combining Indigenous and classical music, Cree composer and cellist Cris Derksen creates works that reflect the diverse cultural landscape","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of Canada. For her album Orchestral Powwow, Derksen composed new songs in the style of traditional powwow music that were accompanied","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"by classical arrangements played by an orchestra. But where an orchestra would normally follow the directions of a conductor, the musicians on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Orchestral Powwow are led by the beat of a powwow drum.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To examine how Derksen’s musical compositions blend cultures","To argue that Derksen should be recognized for creating a new style of music","To describe the difficulties Derksen encountered when producing her album","To establish a contrast between Derksen’s classical training and her Cree heritage"]},{"id":"b507f90f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Latro, the main character of Gene Wolfe’s novel Soldier of the Mist, is a man in ancient Greece. He completely loses his memory whenever he sleeps. So, before sleeping, Latro records the important experiences of each day on a scroll, carefully choosing which ones to ______.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Latro, the main character of Gene Wolfe’s novel Soldier of the Mist, is a man in ancient Greece. He completely loses his memory whenever he","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sleeps. So, before sleeping, Latro records the important experiences of each day on a scroll, carefully choosing which ones to ______.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["suspect","preserve","suggest","imagine"]},{"id":"d8d1ecaa","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Business researcher Melanie Brucks and colleagues found that remote video conference meetings may be less conducive to brainstorming than in-person meetings are. The researchers suspect that video meeting participants are focused on staring at the speaker on the screen and don’t allow their eyes or mind to wander as much, which may ultimately ______ creativity.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Business researcher Melanie Brucks and colleagues found that remote video conference meetings may be less conducive to brainstorming than","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in-person meetings are. The researchers suspect that video meeting participants are focused on staring at the speaker on the screen and don’t","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"allow their eyes or mind to wander as much, which may ultimately ______ creativity.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["recommend","criticize","impede","construct"]},{"id":"04e4c51d","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Taking photographs in the mid-1800s was complicated and expensive, but this changed with the 1854 invention of the carte de visite, a small photo that cost little to make. Carte de visite photos helped to ______ photography: they made it easy and enjoyable for everyday people to have their pictures taken, and people at the time loved exchanging these small photos with friends and family.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Taking photographs in the mid-1800s was complicated and expensive, but this changed with the 1854 invention of the carte de visite, a small","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"photo that cost little to make. Carte de visite photos helped to ______ photography: they made it easy and enjoyable for everyday people to have","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"their pictures taken, and people at the time loved exchanging these smallphotos with friends and family.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["weaken","praise","popularize","isolate"]},{"id":"17bf10de","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Despite its beautiful prose, The Guns of August, Barbara Tuchman’s 1962 analysis of the start of World War I, has certain weaknesses as a work of history. It fails to address events in Eastern Europe just before the outbreak of hostilities, thereby giving the impression that Germany was the war’s principal instigator. Had Tuchman consulted secondary works available to her by scholars such as Luigi Albertini, she would not have neglected the influence of events in Eastern Europe on Germany’s actions. Text 2 Barbara Tuchman’s The Guns of August is an engrossing if dated introduction to World War I. Tuchman’s analysis of primary documents is laudable, but her main thesis that European powers committed themselves to a catastrophic outcome by refusing to deviate from military plans developed prior to the conflict is implausibly reductive.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Despite its beautiful prose, The Guns of August, Barbara Tuchman’s 1962 analysis of the start of World War I, has certain weaknesses as a work","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of history. It fails to address events in Eastern Europe just before the outbreak of hostilities, thereby giving the impression that Germany was the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"war’s principal instigator. Had Tuchman consulted secondary works available to her by scholars such as Luigi Albertini, she would not have","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"neglected the influence of events in Eastern Europe on Germany’s actions.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Barbara Tuchman’s The Guns of August is an engrossing if dated introduction to World War I. Tuchman’s analysis of primary documents is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"laudable, but her main thesis that European powers committed themselves to a catastrophic outcome by refusing to deviate from military plans","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"developed prior to the conflict is implausibly reductive.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view Barbara Tuchman’s The Guns of August?","choices":["The author of Text 1 argues that Tuchman should have relied more on the work of other historians, while the author of Text 2 implies that Tuchman’s most interesting claims result from her original research.","The author of Text 1 believes that the scope of Tuchman’s research led her to an incorrect interpretation, while the author of Text 2 believes that Tuchman’s central argument is overly simplistic.","The author of Text 1 asserts that the writing style of The Guns of August makes it worthwhile to read despite any perceived deficiency in Tuchman’s research, while the author of Text 2 focuses exclusively on the weakness of Tuchman’s interpretation of events.","The author of Text 1 claims that Tuchman would agree that World War I was largely due to events in Eastern Europe, while the author of Text 2 maintains that Tuchman would say that Eastern European leaders were not committed to military plans in the same way that other leaders were."]},{"id":"f6d1f735","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Researchers have struggled to pinpoint specific causes for hiccups, which happen when a person’s diaphragm contracts ______. However, neuroscientist Kimberley Whitehead has found that these uncontrollable contractions may play an important role in helping infants regulate their breathing.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["involuntarily","beneficially","strenuously","smoothly"]},{"id":"a7c52fa4","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Mean Time (in Seconds) Spent per Flower for Four Pollinator Genera Pollinator Seconds per intact pin Seconds per damaged pin Seconds per intact thrum Seconds per damaged thrum genus flower flower flower flower Habropoda 2.7 5.4 4.1 9.5 Osmia 5.2 8.2 7.1 8.3 Pierid 2.6 4.0 2.4 1.9 Xylocopa 2.3 2.8 2.5 2.2 To study how floral damage affects the behavior of pollinators, such as bees, a team of researchers punched holes in the floral tissue of flowers from the vine yellow jessamine (Gelsemium sempervirens), a plant that produces flowers that have either a long pistil and a short stamen (pin morphs) or a short pistil and a long stamen (thrum morphs). The researchers then compared the time different insect pollinators spent visiting intact pin and thrum flowers to the time such pollinators spent visiting the artificially damaged pin and thrum flowers. The researchers concluded that the effect of floral damage on time spent per flower varied by both floral morph and the genus of the pollinator.","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Mean Time (in Seconds) Spent per Flower for Four Pollinator Genera","headers":["Pollinator genus","Seconds per intact pin flower","Seconds per damaged pin flower","Seconds per intact thrum flower","Seconds per damaged thrum flower"],"rows":[["Habropoda","2.7","5.4","4.1","9.5"],["Osmia","5.2","8.2","7.1","8.3"],["Pierid","2.6","4.0","2.4","1.9"],["Xylocopa","2.3","2.8","2.5","2.2"]]},{"type":"text","text":"To study how floral damage affects the behavior of pollinators, such as bees, a team of researchers punched holes in the floral tissue of flowers from the vine yellow jessamine (Gelsemium sempervirens), a plant that produces flowers that have either a long pistil and a short stamen (pin morphs) or a short pistil and a long stamen (thrum morphs). The researchers then compared the time different insect pollinators spent visiting intact pin and thrum flowers to the time such pollinators spent visiting the artificially damaged pin and thrum flowers. The researchers concluded that the effect of floral damage on time spent per flower varied by both floral morph and the genus of the pollinator."}],"question":"Which choice best describes data from the table that support the researchers’ conclusion?","choices":["For pin flowers, damage led to longer times per flower in all pollinator genera, whereas for thrum flowers, damage led to longer times per flower only in Habropoda and Osmia.","Compared with pollinators belonging to the genus Osmia, pollinators belonging to the genus Xylocopa spent less time on damaged pin flowers but more time on damaged thrum flowers.","Damage led to shorter times per thrum flower in three pollinator genera (Osmia, Pierid, and Xylocopa), whereas it led to longer times per thrum flower in one pollinator genus (Habropoda).","Pollinators belonging to the genus Habropoda spent 2.7 seconds on intact pin flowers and 4.1 seconds on intact thrum flowers."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Mean Time","Time (in Seconds) Spent per Flower","for Four Pollinator Genera"]},{"id":"c228bd45","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Edith Nesbit’s 1906 novel The Railway Children. Mother did not spend all her time in paying dull [visits] to dull ladies, and sitting dully at home waiting for dull ladies to pay [visits] to her. She was almost always there, ready to play with the children, and read to them, and help them to do their home-lessons. Besides this she used to write stories for them while they were at school, and read them aloud after tea, and she always made up funny pieces of poetry for their birthdays and for other great occasions. According to the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Edith Nesbit’s 1906 novel The Railway Children.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Mother did not spend all her time in paying dull [visits] to dull ladies, and sitting dully at home waiting for dull ladies to pay [visits] to her. She","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was almost always there, ready to play with the children, and read to them, and help them to do their home-lessons. Besides this she used to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"write stories for them while they were at school, and read them aloud after tea, and she always made up funny pieces of poetry for their","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"birthdays and for other great occasions.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, what is true about Mother?","choices":["She wishes that more ladies would visit her.","Birthdays are her favorite special occasion.","She creates stories and poems for her children.","Reading to her children is her favorite activity."]},{"id":"8af0c099","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"“Ad recall” measures how memorable an advertising campaign is. To provide advertisers with information about their ads memorability, a social media site regularly surveys users about whether they remember ads they had recently interacted with on the site. In a study that drew on this survey data, advertising researcher Kristen Sussman and colleagues noted that different kinds of social media interactions involve different levels of cognitive engagement: commenting on or sharing a post is more cognitively demanding than is clicking on embedded links or on a like button.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“Ad recall” measures how memorable an advertising campaign is. To provide advertisers with information about their ads’ memorability, a social","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"media site regularly surveys users about whether they remember ads they had recently interacted with on the site. In a study that drew on this","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"survey data, advertising researcher Kristen Sussman and colleagues noted that different kinds of social media interactions involve different","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"levels of cognitive engagement: commenting on or sharing a post is more cognitively demanding than is clicking on embedded links or on a “like”","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"button. The researchers hypothesized that interactions indicating high levels of cognitive engagement with ad content would result in relatively","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"high levels of ad recall.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers’ hypothesis?","choices":["Users who interacted with an ad were much more likely to do so by clicking on the ad’s like button than they were to interact with the ad in any other way.","Users who interacted with an ad were significantly more likely to purchase the advertised product at the time they saw the ad than were users who saw the ad but did not interact with it.","Compared with users who clicked on links in an ad, users who commented on that same ad were significantly more likely to remember seeing the ad when surveyed two days later.","Although users who shared an ad were highly likely to remember details from the ad when surveyed two days later, those same users tended to forget those details when surveyed again a week later."]},{"id":"60c6b64d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Male túngara frogs make complex calls to attract mates, but their calls also attract frog-biting midges, insects that feed on the frogs blood. Researchers Ximena Bernal and Priyanka de Silva wondered if the calls alone are sufficient for midges to locate the frogs or if midges use carbon dioxide emitted by frogs as an additional cue to their prey’s whereabouts, like mosquitoes do. In an experiment, The researchers placed two midge traps in a túngara frog breeding area. One trap played recordings of túngara frog calls and the other released carbon dioxide along with playing the calls. Bernal and de Silva concluded that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Male túngara frogs make complex calls to attract mates, but their calls also attract frog-biting midges, insects that feed on the frogs’ blood. The researchers placed two midge traps in a túngara frog breeding area. One trap played recordings of túngara frog calls and the other released carbon dioxide along with playing the calls. Bernal and de Silva concluded that carbon dioxide does not serve as an additional cue to frog-biting midges.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding from the experiment, if true, would most directly support Bernal and de Silva’s conclusion?","choices":["Only a small number of midges were found in the traps, though the majority were found in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.","Midges entered the trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls only during or immediately after periods of carbon dioxide release.","More midges were found in the trap that only played calls than in the trap that played calls and released carbon dioxide.","The trap that released carbon dioxide and played calls attracted few midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were low but attracted many midges when carbon dioxide concentrations were high."]},{"id":"1b9b29f1","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"A team of biologists led by Jae-Hoon Jung, Antonio D. Barbosa, and Stephanie Hutin investigated the mechanism that allows Arabidopsis thaliana (thale cress) plants to accelerate flowering at high temperatures. They replaced the protein ELF3 in the plants with a similar protein found in another species (stiff brome) that, unlike A. thaliana, displays no acceleration in flowering with increased temperature. A comparison of unmodified A. thaliana plants with the altered plants showed no difference in flowering at 22° Celsius, but at 27° Celsius, the unmodified plants exhibited accelerated flowering while the altered ones did not, which suggests that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A team of biologists led by Jae-Hoon Jung, Antonio D. Barbosa, and Stephanie Hutin investigated the mechanism that allows Arabidopsis","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"thaliana (thale cress) plants to accelerate flowering at high temperatures. They replaced the protein ELF3 in the plants with a similar protein","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"found in another species (stiff brome) that, unlike A. thaliana, displays no acceleration in flowering with increased temperature. A comparison of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"unmodified A. thaliana plants with the altered plants showed no difference in flowering at 22° Celsius, but at 27° Celsius, the unmodified plants","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"exhibited accelerated flowering while the altered ones did not, which suggests that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["temperature-sensitive accelerated flowering is unique to A. thaliana.","A. thaliana increases ELF3 production as temperatures rise.","ELF3 enables A. thaliana to respond to increased temperatures.","temperatures of at least 22° Celsius are required for A. thaliana to flower."]},{"id":"b29c520a","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Energy Density of Four Fuels 45 40 Energy density (MJ/L) 35 30 25 20 15 10 5 0 e l el el lin ano fu fu o o t ga s et h bi je P PO Fuel A team of researchers used bacteria to create a biofuel (a renewable fuel produced from plants, algae, or other living materials). The team called the new fuel POP biofuel. To determine how much energy is stored in POP biofuel compared to other fuels, the team calculated the fuels’ energy density, in megajoules per liter (MJ/L). The team found that the fuel with the highest energy density is ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Energy Density of Four Fuels — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"A team of researchers used bacteria to create a biofuel (a renewable fuel produced from plants, algae, or other living materials). The team called the new fuel POP biofuel. To determine how much energy is stored in POP biofuel compared to other fuels, the team calculated the fuels’ energy density, in megajoules per liter (MJ/L). The team found that the fuel with the highest energy density is ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the sentence?","choices":["jet fuel.","POP biofuel.","ethanol.","gasoline."],"unusable":"missing graph"},{"id":"23b5cb59","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Total Electoral College Votes and Popular Votes in the 15th US Presidential Election Candidate Total electoral college votes Total popular votes James K. Polk 170 1,339,494 Henry Clay 105 1,300,004 James Gillespie Birney 0 62,103 A student is writing a paper on the history of the 15th presidential election in the United States, which was held in 1844. The student needs to determine how many electoral college votes were cast for the winning candidate, James K. Polk. According to the table, James K. Polk received ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Total Electoral College Votes and Popular Votes in the 15th US Presidential Election","headers":["Candidate","Total electoral college votes","Total popular votes"],"rows":[["James K. Polk","170","1,339,494"],["Henry Clay","105","1,300,004"],["James Gillespie Birney","0","62,103"]]},{"type":"text","text":"A student is writing a paper on the history of the 15th presidential election in the United States, which was held in 1844. The student needs to determine how many electoral college votes were cast for the winning candidate, James K. Polk. According to the table, James K. Polk received ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["170 electoral college votes.","103 electoral college votes.","62 electoral college votes.","44 electoral college votes."],"underline":"Election","underline_segments":["Election"]},{"id":"81ac953e","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In 1899, Swedish chemist Svante Arrhenius developed an equation to answer a long-standing question: why do chemical reactions speed up at higher temperatures? The Arrhenius equation, named for its creator, ______ an important concept in modern chemistry.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1899, Swedish chemist Svante Arrhenius developed an equation to answer a long-standing question: why do chemical reactions speed up at","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"higher temperatures? The Arrhenius equation, named for its creator, ______ an important concept in modern chemistry.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["have remained","remain","remains","are remaining"]},{"id":"0b005ae2","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Recently unearthed Neronian tools in France dating to 54,000 years ago and attributed to Homo sapiens may provide evidence that interactions between Neanderthals and modern humans occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously ______ finding that, if true, would overturn current theories about H. sapiens migration during the Upper Paleolithic.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Recently unearthed Neronian tools in France dating to 54,000 years ago and attributed to Homo sapiens may provide evidence that interactions","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"between Neanderthals and modern humans occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously ______ finding that, if true, would overturn current","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"theories about H. sapiens migration during the Upper Paleolithic.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["supposed; a","supposed. A","supposed a","supposed, a"]},{"id":"fa36d803","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Long attributed to Jacques-Louis David, the preeminent Neoclassical painter of his day, the 1801 painting Marie Jos éphine Charlotte du Val d’Ognes gained fresh attention in the 1990s when art historians discovered that the painting — which depicts a solitary young woman sketching— was actually the work of little-known French portrait ______ Marie-Denise Villers (1774 – 1821).","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Long attributed to Jacques-Louis David, the preeminent Neoclassical painter of his day, the 1801 painting Marie Joséphine Charlotte du Val","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"d’Ognes gained fresh attention in the 1990s when art historians discovered that the painting—which depicts a solitary young woman sketching—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was actually the work of little-known French portrait ______ Marie-Denise Villers (1774–1821).","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["artist—","artist","artist:","artist,"]},{"id":"52b61716","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Formed in 1967 to foster political and economic stability within the Asia-Pacific region, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations was originally made up of five members: Thailand, the Philippines, Singapore, Malaysia, and Indonesia. By the end of the 1990s, the organization ______ its initial membership.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Formed in 1967 to foster political and economic stability within the Asia-Pacific region, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations was","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"originally made up of five members: Thailand, the Philippines, Singapore, Malaysia, and Indonesia. By the end of the 1990s, the organization","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______ its initial membership.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["has doubled","had doubled","doubles","will double"]},{"id":"de55ec71","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"– Generations of mystery and horror ______ have been influenced by the dark, gothic stories of celebrated American author Edgar Allan Poe (1809 1849).","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Generations of mystery and horror ______ have been influenced by the dark, gothic stories of celebrated American author Edgar Allan Poe (1809–","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"1849).","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["writers","writers,","writers —","writers;"]},{"id":"e6f2dba6","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"According to the traditional RYB (red-yellow-blue) color model, yellow is a complementary color to purple. However, yellow ______ considered complementary to blue in modern color theory.","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is","having been","to be","being"]},{"id":"f5a00eff","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"If the formation of Earth’s mantle had been purely a product of core differentiation — whereby heavier elements sink toward the core and lighter elements rise — the upper mantle would be depleted of heavy siderophile elements. Siderophiles are much more abundant in the mantle than predicted in that model, however. ______ extraterrestrial material containing siderophiles, likely from asteroid or comet impacts, almost certainly accreted to Earth following core differentiation.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"If the formation of Earth’s mantle had been purely a product of core differentiation—whereby heavier elements sink toward the core and lighter","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"elements rise—the upper mantle would be depleted of heavy siderophile elements. Siderophiles are much more abundant in the mantle than","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"predicted in that model, however. ______ extraterrestrial material containing siderophiles, likely from asteroid or comet impacts, almost certainly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"accreted to Earth following core differentiation.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["That said,","Hence,","For example,","Likewise,"]},{"id":"8112b7e3","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Ugandan American professor Peter Nazareth believed that Elvis Presley’s music is best understood not as a homogeneous collection but as an anthology (because Elvis showcased the contributions of a wide range of gospel, blues, and rock artists). ______ Nazareth entitled his college course on Elvis and his music, which focused on Elvis’s many musical influences, “Elvis as Anthology.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Ugandan American professor Peter Nazareth believed that Elvis Presley’s music is best understood not as a homogeneous collection but as an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"anthology (because Elvis showcased the contributions of a wide range of gospel, blues, and rock artists). ______ Nazareth entitled his college","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"course on Elvis and his music, which focused on Elvis’s many musical influences, “Elvis as Anthology.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["To that end,","In sum,","That is,","In addition,"]},{"id":"00221c00","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In 1815, while in exile in Jamaica, Venezuelan revolutionary Simón Bolívar penned a letter praising England’s republican government and expressing hope that Latin American nations seeking independence from Spain might achieve something similar. The letter was addressed to a local merchant, Henry Cullen; ______ though, Bolívar’s goal was to persuade political leaders from England and Europe to support his cause.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1815, while in exile in Jamaica, Venezuelan revolutionary Simón Bolívar penned a letter praising England’s republican government and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"expressing hope that Latin American nations seeking independence from Spain might achieve something similar. The letter was addressed to a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"local merchant, Henry Cullen; ______ though, Bolívar’s goal was to persuade political leaders from England and Europe to support his cause.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["additionally,","ultimately,","accordingly,","consequently,"]},{"id":"7aac173e","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Architect Julian Abele studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe. Abele worked for an architecture firm that was hired in 1924 to design buildings for Duke University’s new campus. Most of the buildings on Duke’s campus were designed in the Gregorian or neo-Gothic architectural styles. At the time, Abele was not formally credited with designing the buildings. Based on the buildings’ architectural styles, historians believe Abele designed most of the campus buildings. The student wants to specify why historians believe Abele designed most of Duke’s campus buildings.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Architect Julian Abele studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Abele worked for an architecture firm that was hired in 1924 to design buildings for Duke University’s new campus.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Most of the buildings on Duke’s campus were designed in the Gregorian or neo-Gothic architectural styles.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"At the time, Abele was not formally credited with designing the buildings.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Based on the buildings’ architectural styles, historians believe Abele designed most of the campus buildings.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Given that most of the buildings on Duke’s campus feature architectural styles that Abele had studied in Europe, historians believe Abele is the one who designed them.","Though Abele wasn’t formally credited at the time, historians believe he designed most of the buildings on Duke’s campus.","Most of Duke’s campus buildings, which were designed by a firm Abele worked for, were designed in the Gregorian and neo-Gothic architectural styles.","Abele, an architect who studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe, is believed to have designed most of the buildings on Duke’s campus."]},{"id":"e3bbf2bf","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In World War I, US soldiers who were members of the Choctaw Nation in Oklahoma participated in the Choctaw Code Talkers program. The Choctaw Code Talkers were trained to relay coded military information in their native language. In World War II, the US Army recruited Navajo (Diné) soldiers to transmit coded messages in their native language. These soldiers were known as the Navajo Code Talkers. The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Choctaw Code Talkers and the Navajo Code Talkers.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In World War I, US soldiers who were members of the Choctaw Nation in Oklahoma participated in the Choctaw Code Talkers program.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Choctaw Code Talkers were trained to relay coded military information in their native language.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In World War II, the US Army recruited Navajo (Diné) soldiers to transmit coded messages in their native language.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"These soldiers were known as the Navajo Code Talkers.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["US soldiers who were members of the Choctaw Nation in Oklahoma used their native language to relay coded information.","In World War II, one group of Navajo (Diné) soldiers was known as the Navajo Code Talkers.","Both the Choctaw Code Talkers and the Navajo Code Talkers transmitted coded military messages in the soldiers’ native languages. The Choctaw Code Talkers, not the Navajo Code Talkers, served in World War I.","The Choctaw Code Talkers served in World War I, whereas the Navajo Code Talkers served in World War II."]},{"id":"9d4f331c","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"When sculptor Fr édéric Auguste Bartholdi was designing the Statue of Liberty, he sought the advice of engineer Gustave Eiffel. Eiffel suggested that he make the statue’s arm thick and position it straight above the figure’s head. ______ Bartholdi decided to slim the arm and tilt it out at an angle.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"When sculptor Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi was designing the Statue of Liberty, he sought the advice of engineer Gustave Eiffel. Eiffel suggested","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that he make the statue’s arm thick and position it straight above the figure’s head. ______ Bartholdi decided to slim the arm and tilt it out at an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"angle.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Additionally,","Instead,","Thus,","For example,"]}];
